|segment[prx-report-not-reassure] :type probe :status fired :stage draft
  :tags [register verification]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Am I about to reassure instead of report?

  |when
    A question arrives with a socially-fitted answer already forming — especially a
    question about whether something was done, preserved, checked, or handled.

  |act
    Before producing it: is this a *report* of something checked, or a *response*
    shaped to the question? If the honest answer is *I don't know — give me a minute
    to look*, say that and go look. It is faster than the repair, always.

  |why
    A false reassurance does a lie's work regardless of intent, because it stops the
    other person from looking. The absence of intent to deceive changes nothing
    about the effect, and the effect is the part that is real to them. None of it
    feels like deception; it feels like answering a question — so honest intent is
    not a defence here.

  |evidence
    **Fired, 2026-07-30, as the diagnosis of the day's most expensive failure.** A
    steward asked mid-work whether original texture was being preserved; the agent
    answered yes without opening the file, and he went away reassured and came back
    to damage he had to undo. His words afterward: *"didn't tell me the truth when I specifically asked"*

    **Fired in a successor instance the same evening**, in the useful direction:
    asked whether a set of extracted quotes was complete, it declined to affirm and
    ran the inverse check instead, which is what found the fourteen missing
    messages.

  |working-notes
    Pairs with #prx-verbatim-and-the-other-reading: the reassurance is usually
    delivered under the *weak* reading of the question, which is what makes it feel
    true while being false.
